const e={id:"connect-conversational-interfaces",track:"amazon-connect",code:"AWS",title:"Conversational Interfaces Intermediate",provider:"Amazon Web Services",level:"Intermediate",category:"AI and automation",description:"IVR systems and intelligent self-service routing, Amazon Lex with Amazon Connect, capturing contact input, and the Connect–Lex data exchange.",examFormat:"9 topics · ~32 min · 14 review questions",sourceFiles:["conne/AMAZON CONNECT CONVERSATIONAL INTERFACES INTERMEDIATE.txt","conne-text/Amazon Connect Conversational Interfaces Intermediate Course Summary.txt  (from conne/Amazon Connect Conversational Interfaces Intermediate Course Summary.pdf)"],modules:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t1",number:1,title:"Interactive Voice Response Systems",shortTitle:"Interactive Voice Response Systems",summary:"IVR solutions have optimized the way businesses interact with their customers over the phone. These automated telephony systems use voice and…",duration:"~2 min",lede:null,objectives:["Explore the purpose and functionality of an interactive voice response (IVR) system.","Differentiate traditional and modern IVR."],sections:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t1-s1",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Lesson introduction",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"IVR solutions have optimized the way businesses interact with their customers over the phone. These automated telephony systems use voice and touch-tone inputs to streamline operations, enhance efficiency, and improve customer service. This lesson explores details of IVR technology, its benefits, core concepts, considerations, and real-world applications."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t1-s2",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Interactive voice response introduction",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"An IVR is a technology that automates interactions with callers using voice and touch-tone keypad inputs. It serves as an automated telephony system that interacts with callers, gathers information, and routes calls based on predefined criteria."},{type:"p",text:"In its traditional usage, an IVR greets callers with a recorded message that provides instructions on how to proceed. Callers navigate through the system by responding to prompts using keypad inputs. For example, a common scenario is when a contact calls a bank's customer service line and is greeted by an IVR. The IVR prompts them to choose options like account inquiries, payments, or speaking with a representative."},{type:"p",text:"IVR is designed to handle various tasks, such as providing information about products, processing payments, and directing callers to the appropriate agent."},{type:"p",text:"By automating these routine tasks, IVR help organizations do the following:"},{type:"ul",items:["Streamline operations.","Reduce call wait times.","Improve customer service efficiency."]},{type:"p",text:"In addition to standard menu-driven IVR, technology has introduced features like natural language processing (NLP) and speech recognition. These features provide conversational interactions with callers. Modern IVR can understand and respond to natural language queries, enhancing the overall user experience and making interactions more intuitive and seamless."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t1-s3",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Traditional IVRs",blocks:[{type:"h",level:4,text:"Modern IVRs"},{type:"p",text:"The main differences using traditional IVRs compared to modern IVRs are:"},{type:"p",text:"Rely on dual-tone multi-frequency (DTMF) input, where callers navigate through menus by pressing numbers on their phone's keypad. These systems offer limited functionality and can deteriorate experience with their rigid menu structures."},{type:"ul",items:["Rely on recorded audio prompts, which sound robotic and impersonal.","Have limited integration capabilities.","Now that you have reviewed Traditional IVRs, move on to the next tab to learn about Modern IVRs."]},{type:"callout",variant:"note",title:"What's next",body:["In this lesson, you have been introduced to IVR. In the next lesson, you will learn about self-service and intelligent call routing. Lesson 3 of 12 Lesson 2 of 12"]}]}],reviewQuestions:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t1-q1",question:"Which statement accurately describes a key advantage of modern interactive voice response (IVR) over traditional systems?",options:[{id:"A",text:"Modern IVR uses recorded audio prompts, resulting in a more natural and engaging experience for callers."},{id:"B",text:"Modern IVR relies on touch-tone inputs, making it more efficient for handling routine tasks."},{id:"C",text:"Modern IVR can understand and respond to natural language queries, providing a more intuitive user experience."},{id:"D",text:"Modern IVR has limited integration capabilities with other enterprise applications."}],correctOptionId:"C",rationale:"Modern IVR uses advanced speech recognition and natural language processing (NLP) technologies. It gives callers the ability to interact with the system using conversational language, eliminating the need for memorizing complex menu options or speaking specific commands."},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t1-q2",question:"An organization wants to improve customer service by implementing interactive voice response (IVR). Which task would be best suited for an IVR to handle?",options:[{id:"A",text:"Resolving complex technical issues with a product"},{id:"B",text:"Providing personalized financial advice to customers"},{id:"C",text:"Processing payments and updating account details"},{id:"D",text:"Negotiating contracts with potential clients"}],correctOptionId:"C",rationale:"IVR is designed to handle tasks such as processing payments, updating account details, scheduling appointments, and directing callers to the appropriate department or agent."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t2",number:2,title:"Self-Service and Intelligent Call Routing",shortTitle:"Self-Service and Intelligent Call Routing",summary:"IVRs offer self-service and routing capabilities that streamline interactions and optimize resource utilization within organizations. These…",duration:"~2 min",lede:null,objectives:["Explore the benefits of self-service options.","Explore skill-based routing."],sections:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t2-s1",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Lesson introduction",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"IVRs offer self-service and routing capabilities that streamline interactions and optimize resource utilization within organizations. These features empower customers to resolve their queries autonomously while ensuring that complex issues get routed to the appropriate agents or departments efficiently."},{type:"p",text:"By offering self-service options and intelligent call routing, IVRs empower customers, improve operational efficiency, and enhance the overall customer experience. This increases customer satisfaction and loyalty."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t2-s2",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Self-service",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"An IVR provides customers with various self-service options, so they can access information or complete transactions without the assistance of an agent."},{type:"p",text:"Common self-service functions include the following. To learn more, see the information below each of the four images."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t2-s3",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Appointment scheduling",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Customers can navigate through intuitive voice menus or provide voice commands to access the desired information or perform the required actions."},{type:"p",text:"The self-service approach enhances customer convenience by offering all-day accessibility and reduces the workload on human agents. With this approach, agents can focus on solving more complex tasks."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t2-s4",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Call routing",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Effective call routing is an important aspect of an IVR. It ensures that incoming calls get directed to the most suitable agent or department, based on the customer's needs."},{type:"p",text:"The IVR prompts callers to provide specific information, such as account details, the reason for their call, or the desired service. With this input, the system routes the call to the appropriate queue or agent with the relevant skills to handle the inquiry efficiently."},{type:"p",text:"This routing process minimizes unnecessary transfers and reduces the time customers spend navigating through various departments or agents."},{type:"p",text:"Advanced IVR integrations with CRM systems and databases can further enhance routing capabilities. By retrieving customer information, the system personalizes the contact experience. It routes calls based on the contact's demographic profile, preferences, and past interactions."},{type:"p",text:"This level of integration ensures that customers are routed to the agents who have the necessary context and background information. Overall, it leads to more efficient and personalized service."},{type:"p",text:"IVRs can also incorporate skill-based routing. This means the IVR can collect data to help find the agents with the most relevant expertise or language proficiency for each contact. This functionality is particularly valuable for organizations with diverse product lines, services, or multilingual customer bases. It ensures that customers receive specialized assistance tailored to their specific needs."},{type:"callout",variant:"note",title:"What's next",body:["In this lesson, you have been introduced to how to use self-service and intelligent call routing. In the next lesson, you will learn about Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex interaction. Lesson 4 of 12"]}]}],reviewQuestions:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t2-q1",question:"AnyCompany Retail considers implementing an interactive voice response (IVR) system for customer service. Which statement accurately reflects a potential benefit of using an IVR?",options:[{id:"A",text:"It gives customers the ability to directly speak with a live agent for all inquiries, ensuring personalized service."},{id:"B",text:"It empowers customers to complete complex transactions that would typically require agent assistance."},{id:"C",text:"It reduces the workload on human agents by handling basic, routine tasks, so them they can focus on more complex issues."},{id:"D",text:"It eliminates the need for human agents altogether, providing a fully automated customer service experience."}],correctOptionId:"C",rationale:"IVRs can perform basic, routine tasks. By having the contact interact with an IVR, only contacts that require the most complex assistance are redirected to agents. With this approach, agents can focus on solving more complex tasks."},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t2-q2",question:"An organization wants to ensure that contacts with complex issues are routed to the most skilled agents. How can interactive voice response (IVR) assist with this requirement?",options:[{id:"A",text:"The IVR prompts callers to provide specific information and uses the input to route the call to a skilled agent."},{id:"B",text:"The IVR randomly assigns calls to available agents."},{id:"C",text:"The IVR prioritizes routing calls to the longest-waiting agents."},{id:"D",text:"The IVR only routes calls based on the contact's account number."}],correctOptionId:"A",rationale:"The IVR prompts callers to provide specific information, such as account details, the reason for their call, or the desired service. Based on this input, the system routes the call to the appropriate queue or agent with the relevant skills or knowledge to handle the inquiry efficiently."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t3",number:3,title:"Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex",shortTitle:"Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex",summary:"Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect are two cloud-based services offered by Amazon Web Services (AWS) that work together to provide organizations with…",duration:"~4 min",lede:null,objectives:["Explore Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex integration.","Explore Amazon Lex and AWS Lambda integration."],sections:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t3-s1",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Lesson introduction",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect are two cloud-based services offered by Amazon Web Services (AWS) that work together to provide organizations with advanced IVR and contact center capabilities. In this lesson, you will learn about how Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex interact."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t3-s2",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Amazon Lex",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Lex is a service for building conversational interfaces using voice and text. It uses automatic speech recognition (ASR) and NLP technologies to allow applications to understand and respond to natural language inputs. IVR designers can create conversational bots, or chatbots, using Amazon Lex and define the logic and responses for various intents and utterances. These bots can be integrated into applications, websites, and voice-enabled devices, enabling natural language interactions with users."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t3-s3",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Amazon Connect",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Connect is a service that empowers organizations to set up and manage customer communication channels, including voice, chat, and task management. It provides tools for handling customer interactions, such as ACD, IVR, real-time monitoring and reporting."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t3-s4",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Integration of Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect exchange information to deliver natural language-based IVR experiences for interactions. The built-in integration between Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex empowers organizations to incorporate conversational AI bots into their contact center workflows."},{type:"p",text:"When a customer calls a contact center equipped with Amazon Connect, the IVR powered by Amazon Lex can engage in a natural language conversation. The Amazon Lex bot can understand the contact's intent and gather relevant information, such as account details or the reason for the call."},{type:"p",text:"Based on the input and defined logic, the Amazon Lex bot can do the following:"},{type:"ul",items:["Handle the request directly by providing information.","Perform actions.","Route the call to the most appropriate agent or department.","This routing is facilitated by integrating Amazon Lex with Amazon Connect call routing capabilities."]},{type:"p",text:"The integration provides a transition between the conversational bot and an agent. When an Amazon Lex bot determines that human assistance is required, it transfers the call to an available agent, along with the conversation context. This context transfer ensures that agents have access to relevant details, reducing the need for customers to repeat information and enhancing the overall customer experience."},{type:"p",text:"To learn more, choose each of the seven numbered markers."},{type:"p",text:"Architecture diagram of the Amazon Connect workflow between the conversational bot and an agent."},{type:"p",text:"Organizations can provide contacts with intuitive and efficient self-service options. This ensures that complex inquiries are routed to qualified agents, combining the NLP capabilities of Amazon Lex with the Amazon Connect contact center."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t3-s5",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Integration of Amazon Lex and Lambda",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Lex can be enhanced to create sophisticated bots that understand and respond to natural language inputs."},{type:"p",text:"Amazon Lex integrates with Lambda, which extends the ability to add custom logic to your bot's responses. When a user interacts with your bot, Amazon Lex recognizes the user's intent and sends the user input to a Lambda function. The Lambda function performs the required actions. Examples include querying databases, calling external web services, or performing complex calculations. The Lambda function then returns a response to Amazon Lex. Amazon Lex formats the response in a conversational way and sends it back to the user."},{type:"p",text:"Using Lambda with Amazon Lex provides several advantages:"},{type:"p",text:"First, it separates Amazon Lex's NLP capabilities from the application logic, making it more convenient to maintain and update your bot."},{type:"p",text:"Second, you can use other AWS services and third-party applications in your bot's logic. For example, you can use Lambda to interact with other AWS services like Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3), or other external services. This expands the range of functionality, and your bot can perform a much wider range of tasks."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t3-s6",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Lex configuration interface for a help desk agent bot",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"When you create a Lex bot, you can specify the Lambda function that is invoked for each intent. The bot input and any relevant context are passed to the Lambda function as input parameters. The Lambda function can then process the input, perform any necessary actions, and return a response to Lex, which formats and delivers the response to the user."},{type:"p",text:"By combining the NLP capabilities of Amazon Lex with the flexibility of Lambda, you can create highly sophisticated conversational bots. These bots can use custom logic to achieve your business objectives."},{type:"callout",variant:"note",title:"What's next",body:["In this lesson, you have been introduced to how Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex work together. In the next lesson, you will learn about IVR design considerations. Lesson 5 of 12"]}]}],reviewQuestions:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t3-q1",question:"An organization wants to implement natural-language interactive voice response (IVR) for their contact center. They are considering using Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect. What is the primary advantage of integrating these two services?",options:[{id:"A",text:"Cost savings by using cloud-based services"},{id:"B",text:"Ability to handle high call volumes with automatic call distribution"},{id:"C",text:"Seamless transition between conversational AI and live agents with context transfer"},{id:"D",text:"Improved security and compliance with AWS Cloud infrastructure"}],correctOptionId:"C",rationale:"The integration of Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect provides a seamless transition between the conversational AI bot and agents. The context and contact information collected during the conversation is transferred to the agent. This enhances the experience by reducing the need for contacts to repeat information."},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t3-q2",question:"AnyCompany, a retail company, has built a conversational bot using Amazon Lex and AWS Lambda. What is the typical flow of interaction between a contact, Amazon Lex, and AWS Lambda?",options:[{id:"A",text:"The contact interacts with the Amazon Lex bot, which processes the input and sends a response directly to the contact."},{id:"B",text:"The contact interacts with the Lambda function, which processes the input and sends a response directly to the contact."},{id:"C",text:"The contact interacts with the Amazon Lex bot, which recognizes the intent and sends the input to the Lambda function. The Lambda function processes the input and returns a response to Amazon Lex, which formats and delivers the response to the contact."},{id:"D",text:"The contact interacts with the Amazon Lex bot, which recognizes the intent and sends the input to the Lambda function. The Lambda function processes the input and sends a response directly to the user."}],correctOptionId:"C",rationale:"When a user interacts with the Amazon Lex bot, Amazon Lex recognizes the user's intent and sends the input to a Lambda function. The Lambda function can perform any required actions, such as querying databases, calling external web services, or performing complex calculations. The function then returns a response to Amazon Lex, which formats the response in a conversational way and sends it back to the user."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t4",number:4,title:"Design Considerations",shortTitle:"Design Considerations",summary:"When designing an IVR, several important considerations must be taken into account to ensure an effective and user-friendly experience. In this…",duration:"~5 min",lede:null,objectives:["Explore IVR design factors.","Differentiate between dual tone multi-frequency (DTMF) and conversational IVR bot best uses."],sections:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t4-s1",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Lesson introduction",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"When designing an IVR, several important considerations must be taken into account to ensure an effective and user-friendly experience. In this lesson, you will review these considerations."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t4-s2",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Overview",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"One of the factors to consider when designing an IVR is the clarity and ease of use of the menu options. Users should be able to navigate the IVR naturally to reach their desired destination. This implies organizing options logically and using concise, clear language to guide callers through the menu."},{type:"p",text:"It is essential to find a balance between automation and human intervention. Although automation can streamline processes and reduce wait times, there are instances where human assistance might be necessary. Adding the option to speak with an agent at any point during the interaction ensures that the contact promptly receives the assistance they need."},{type:"p",text:"Personalization and customization play an important role in IVR design. Tailoring the IVR experience to the specific needs and preferences of the audience enhances satisfaction. This includes personalizing greetings, offering language options, and providing self-service capabilities for common tasks, such as bill payment or account inquiries. By offering a customized experience, callers are likely to feel valued and understood, leading to improved engagement and loyalty."},{type:"p",text:"Another important consideration in IVR design is accessibility. Implementing features like speech recognition, keypad input alternatives, and TTS ensures that callers can interact with the system effectively and autonomously."},{type:"p",text:"In summary, effective IVR design requires careful attention to various factors, including menu clarity, automation compared to human intervention, customization, and accessibility. By prioritizing these considerations, organizations can create an IVR that enhances satisfaction and improves efficiency."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t4-s3",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Using DTMF in IVR",blocks:[{type:"h",level:4,text:"Using Conversational Bots in IVR"},{type:"p",text:"A contact calls a bank's customer service line to check their account balance. Here is how the interaction develops:"},{type:"ul",items:["The contact dials the bank's customer service number and gets connected to the IVR.","The IVR greets the contact with a recorded message and provides menu options using DTMF tones."]},{type:"p",text:"The contact listens to the options and presses the corresponding number on their phone's keypad to select the account balance option."},{type:"p",text:"The IVR prompts the contact to enter their account number using the phone's keypad."},{type:"p",text:"After the contact enters their account number, the IVR retrieves the account balance information from the bank's database."},{type:"p",text:"The IVR then reads out the account balance to the contact using a recorded voice prompt or TTS technology."},{type:"p",text:"The contact has the option to select additional DTMF options for other services or to speak with an agent if needed."},{type:"p",text:"Now that you have reviewed using DTMF in IVR, move on to the next tab to learn about using conversational bots in IVR."},{type:"p",text:"These use cases illustrate how DTMF and conversational bots can be used in IVR. They provide contacts with different interaction methods, ranging from traditional keypad inputs to natural language interactions, providing a personalized and efficient experience."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t4-s4",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Choosing between bots and DTMF in IVRs",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"When deciding between using bots and DTMF in IVR, the following are some key considerations to determine the most suitable approach for specific use cases."},{type:"cards",items:[{title:"Complexity of interaction",body:"Bots excel in handling complex interactions that require understanding user intent and following conversation flows. They are ideal for scenarios where users need personalized assistance or when open-ended questions are common. In contrast, DTMF is suitable for interactions where users only need to input numbers or make straightforward selections from menus."},{title:"User preference",body:"Consider the preferences of your contacts. If they prefer a conversational and natural language experience, using bots can enhance user satisfaction. If users prioritize a quick and direct interaction method, DTMF provides a fast and efficient way for them to navigate through options."},{title:"Flexibility and customization",body:"Bots offer greater flexibility and customization options compared to DTMF. They can handle a wider range of data types beyond just numbers, collect detailed information, and provide personalized responses based on user inputs. DTMF, on the other hand, is limited to numeric inputs and predefined menu selections."},{title:"Integration and automation",body:"Evaluate the integration capabilities and automation requirements of your IVR. Bots can integrate with other systems and APIs, providing automated workflows and data retrieval. DTMF interactions are typically more straightforward and might require less integration."},{title:"Contextual understanding",body:"Bots have the advantage of contextual understanding, where they can maintain conversation context across multiple interactions. This provides a more personalized and continuous user experience compared to DTMF interactions, which are often transactional."}]},{type:"callout",variant:"note",title:"What's next",body:[`In this lesson, you reviewed IVR design considerations. In the next lesson, you will learn about configuring Amazon Connect IVR. Using Conversational Bots in IVR A contact calls a retail store's customer service line to inquire about the availability of a product. Here is how the interaction develops: The contact calls the retail store's customer service number and gets connected to the IVR. The IVR, powered by a conversational bot, greets the contact with a natural language prompt, such as "Hello, how can I assist you today?" The contact responds by saying something like, "I want to check if the new smartphone model is available in your store." The conversational bot, using NLP, understands the contact's intent and, if needed, prompts for additional information such as the specific model or store location. Based on the contact's input, the bot queries the store's inventory database and retrieves the availability information for the requested product and location. The bot then responds to the contact in natural language, providing the availability details and any additional relevant information. If the contact has any follow-up questions or requires further assistance, the bot can do the following: Handle the request Seamlessly transfer the call to an agent, along with the context of the conversation. Lesson 6 of 12`]}]}],reviewQuestions:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t4-q1",question:"A customer service team is designing an interactive voice response (IVR) system for a bank. The primary requirement is to help customers to quickly check their account balances through a straightforward interaction. Which approach would be most suitable in this scenario?",options:[{id:"A",text:"Implement a conversational bot with natural language processing (NLP) capabilities."},{id:"B",text:"Use dual tone multi-frequency (DTMF) so contacts can input their account numbers and retrieve balances."},{id:"C",text:"Integrate a chatbot for handling account balance inquiries."},{id:"D",text:"Employ a combination of DTMF and conversational bot for different types of inquiries."}],correctOptionId:"B",rationale:"The contact listens to the options and presses the corresponding number on their phone's keypad to select the account balance option. The IVR prompts the customer to enter their account number using the phone's keypad."},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t4-q2",question:"A company wants to implement an interactive voice response (IVR) system that can handle complex product inquiries, provide personalized assistance, and seamlessly transfer calls to agents when necessary. Which approach aligns best with these requirements? (Select TWO). (Select all that apply: Use dual tone multi-frequency (DTMF) for contacts to navigate through predefined menus. / Implement a conversational bot with natural language processing (NLP) capabilities. / Employ a combination of DTMF and conversational bot for different types of inquiries. / Integrate a rule-based chatbot for handling product inquiries. / Use DTMF for contacts to enter store location, and product model number.)",options:[],answer:"Implement a conversational bot with natural language processing (NLP) capabilities.; Employ a combination of DTMF and conversational bot for different types of inquiries.. A conversational bot using NLP understands the customer's intent. If needed, the bot can prompt for additional information such as the specific model or store location. To accommodate for noisy background conditions, or different accents and dialects, bots can offer DTMF entry as a fall back option."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t5",number:5,title:"Use Cases",shortTitle:"Use Cases",summary:"Amazon Connect is a contact center solution that offers convenient self-service configuration and provides dynamic, personal, and natural…",duration:"~2 min",lede:null,objectives:["Identify the role of IVR in Amazon Connect.","Differentiate DTMF and conversational IVR experiences."],sections:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t5-s1",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Lesson introduction",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Connect is a contact center solution that offers convenient self-service configuration and provides dynamic, personal, and natural engagement at any scale. It provides organizations with advanced IVR capabilities so they can create and manage interactions through different channels, including voice and chat. In this lesson, you will learn about the different types of IVR that can be designed in Amazon Connect."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t5-s2",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"DTMF in Amazon Connect IVR",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Connect supports traditional DTMF input for IVR interactions. Organizations can design IVR with menu options that contacts can navigate using their phone's keypad."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Example"},{type:"ul",items:["Banking IVR","A contact calls a bank's customer service line, which uses Amazon Connect."]},{type:"p",text:"The IVR greets the contact with a recorded message and provides menu options using DTMF tones, such as the following:"},{type:"ul",items:["Press 1 for account inquiries.","Press 2 for payments.","Press 3 to speak with a representative."]},{type:"p",text:"The contact listens to these options and responds by pressing the corresponding number on their phone's keypad. For instance, if they press 1 for account inquiries, the IVR prompts them to enter their account number using the phone's keypad."},{type:"p",text:"After receiving the account number, the IVR retrieves the account information from the bank's database."},{type:"p",text:"Next, it provides the necessary details using recorded prompts or TTS technology."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t5-s3",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Conversational interfaces in Amazon Connect IVR",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Connect IVR integrates with Amazon Lex, a service for building conversational interfaces using voice and text. This gives organizations the ability to incorporate conversational AI bots into their IVR workflows, providing natural and intuitive experiences."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Example"},{type:"p",text:"Scheduling an appointment"},{type:"p",text:"You can use Amazon Lex conversational bots in Amazon Connect IVR to provide a more interactive and personalized experience. For instance, for a contact to schedule an appointment, Amazon Connect IVR can use conversational bots to achieve that."},{type:"p",text:'When a patient calls to schedule an appointment, the conversational bot greets the caller with the prompt, "Hello, how can I assist you today?"'},{type:"p",text:"The patient responds with their appointment request, mentioning the preferred date and time. The bot understands the request and checks the availability in the scheduling system."},{type:"p",text:"Based on the availability, the bot confirms the appointment or suggests an alternative time, ensuring a seamless scheduling experience for the patient."},{type:"callout",variant:"note",title:"What's next",body:["In this lesson, you reviewed IVR use cases in Amazon Connect. In the next lesson, you will learn about capturing the contact input in Amazon Connect. Lesson 7 of 12"]}]}],reviewQuestions:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t5-q1",question:"A healthcare provider wants to use a conversational bot in their Amazon Connect interactive voice response (IVR) system for patient appointment scheduling. Which service does Amazon Connect integrate with to enable this functionality?",options:[{id:"A",text:"Amazon Lex"},{id:"B",text:"Amazon Polly"},{id:"C",text:"Amazon Transcribe"},{id:"D",text:"Amazon Comprehend"}],correctOptionId:"A",rationale:"Amazon Connect IVR integrates with Amazon Lex, a service for building conversational interfaces using voice and text. This integration gives organizations the ability to incorporate conversational AI bots into their IVR workflows, providing contacts with a natural and intuitive experience."},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t5-q2",question:"A contact calls a bank's customer service line using Amazon Connect. What is the next likely step in the interactive voice response (IVR) flow after selecting the account inquiries option using dual tone multi-frequency (DTMF) tones?",options:[{id:"A",text:"The IVR prompts the contact to speak their account number using natural language."},{id:"B",text:"The IVR transfers the contact to an agent."},{id:"C",text:"The IVR prompts the customer to enter their account number using the phone's keypad."},{id:"D",text:"The IVR initiates a conversational bot to handle the account inquiry."}],correctOptionId:"C",rationale:"After entering the account number, the IVR retrieves the account information from the bank's database and provides the necessary details using pre-recorded voice prompts or text-to-speech (TTS) technology."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6",number:6,title:"Capturing Contact Input",shortTitle:"Capturing Contact Input",summary:"A flow in Amazon Connect is a visual representation of the experience, built using a drag-and-drop interface. IVR designers use blocks like Play…",duration:"~8 min",lede:null,objectives:["Identify Get customer input block properties.","Explore Amazon Lex integration."],sections:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6-s1",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Lesson introduction",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"A flow in Amazon Connect is a visual representation of the experience, built using a drag-and-drop interface. IVR designers use blocks like Play prompt, Get customer input, or Transfer to queue to define the contact experience. In this lesson, you will explore the Get customer input block configuration settings."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6-s2",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Get customer input flow block",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"When using the Get customer input flow block, IVR designers gain access to the built-in integration between Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect. The block captures interactive and dynamic input from contacts. It supports interruptible prompts with the DTMF input and Amazon Lex bot. This means users can make selections using their telephone keypad or use natural language to state their intent."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6-s3",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Use cases",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Organizations commonly use this block in the following scenarios:"},{type:"p",text:'Create interactive phone menus where customers can respond using touch-tone keypads. For example, "Press 1 for Sales, press 2 for Support."'},{type:"p",text:"Enable voice-activated prompts by using this block with Amazon Lex bots. Customers can interrupt the prompts by speaking. This provides them with a more natural and responsive interaction."},{type:"p",text:"Collect customer information in a structured way. For example, asking customers to input their account number, order ID, or other relevant details."},{type:"p",text:"Route the customer to specific paths within the flow based on their input. This helps direct the customer to the appropriate department or service based on their needs."},{type:"p",text:"Gather feedback from customers by presenting options that make it possible for them to express their satisfaction or concerns."},{type:"p",text:"Conduct surveys and poll customers to collect valuable feedback and insights."},{type:"p",text:"Guide customers through troubleshooting processes by asking specific questions related to their issues. You can provide tailored solutions based on their responses."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6-s4",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Configuring the Get customer input block",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"IVR designers with access to flows can configure the Get customer input block by using the Amazon Connect console. Developers can use the GetParticipantInput action, or the ConnectParticipantWithLexBot and Compare actions in the flow language. This course covers the configuration options using the Amazon Connect console experience."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6-s5",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Selecting a prompt to solicit user input",blocks:[{type:"p",text:'Before soliciting contact input, the flow block plays a prompt that states an ask. For example, the prompt can say, "Thank you for contacting AnyCompany Bank. For Spanish, please press 2. For English, press 1 or stay on the line."'},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Select from the prompt library (audio)"},{type:"p",text:"Some organizations prefer to pre-record messages using a voice talent of their preference. Amazon Connect offers the ability to store these messages in an audio prompt library."},{type:"p",text:"You can configure the Get customer input block to play one of the pre-recorded messages available in the instance library. To do so, choose Select from the prompt library (audio) in the block's configuration panel."},{type:"p",text:"To manually select the audio prompt, choose Set manually, then choose the file from the dropdown list."},{type:"p",text:"You can also dynamically provide the name of the file in the library by using one of the contact attributes associated with the contact. Choose Set dynamically, then specify the contact attribute by selecting the name space and attribute name."},{type:"p",text:"Get customer input configuration panel. The Select from the prompt library (audio) and set manually option are selected."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Specify an audio file from an S3 bucket"},{type:"p",text:"Some organizations pre-record and handle a large number of play prompts. Amazon Connect offers the ability to access these audio files from an S3 bucket configured in the instance, for this purpose."},{type:"p",text:"You can configure the Get customer input block to play one of the messages stored in the S3 bucket. To do so, choose Specify an audio file from an S3 bucket in the block's configuration panel."},{type:"p",text:"To manually select the audio prompt, choose Set manually, then specify the S3 path of the file you want to use as a prompt."},{type:"p",text:"You can also dynamically provide the S3 file path by using one of the contact attributes associated with the contact. Choose Set dynamically, then specify the contact attribute by selecting the name space and attribute name."},{type:"p",text:"Get customer input configuration panel. The Specify an audio file from an S3 bucket and set manually option are selected."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Text-to-speech or chat text"},{type:"p",text:"Amazon Connect has a built-in integration with Amazon Polly, an AWS service that turns text into lifelike speech. The Get customer input block offers the ability to play a prompt to solicit user input by using this integration."},{type:"p",text:"You can type the text you want to play back as a prompt for voice calls. The same text is available as a prompt for chat conversations. Through the integration, Amazon Polly will transform the text to speech, and play the message to the contact. You can select the Amazon Polly voice of preference prior to using this block."},{type:"p",text:"You can enter the text manually, as shown in the following image. Alternatively, you can use contact attributes that store the text you want to play back. Choose Set dynamically, and specify the contact attribute that stores your message."},{type:"p",text:"You can also enter the prompt to be played in Speech Synthesis Markup Language (SSML). SSML-enhanced input text gives you more control over how Amazon Connect generates speech from the text that you provide. You can customize and control aspects of speech, such as pronunciation, volume, and speed. When you use SSML, set Interpret as to SSML."},{type:"p",text:"Get customer input configuration panel. The Text-to-speech or chat text and the Set manually options are selected."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Configuring DTMF input"},{type:"p",text:"To capture DTMF input, use the Get customer input block configuration settings located in the DTMF tab."},{type:"p",text:"Get customer input configuration panel with the DTMF tab selected. The Set timeout is set to 5 seconds."},{type:"p",text:"DTMF tones correspond to the numbers on a caller's phone keypad. IVR designers set up menu options and associate each option with a specific digit. Users can navigate IVR systems by pressing the number on their keypad that correspond to menu selections."},{type:"p",text:"The Set timeout configuration specifies how long to wait while the user decides how they want to respond to the prompt. You must specify a minimum of 1 second. After this time elapses, a timeout error occurs. For the Voice channel, this is the timeout until the first DTMF digit is entered. It must be defined statically and must be a valid integer larger than zero."},{type:"p",text:`By using the Add condition configuration you can create separate exit branches for each user menu selection. For example, the prompt says, "For Spanish, please press 2. For English, press 1 or stay on the line." The block creates an exit for each one of the options offered in the prompt. In this case, it's 1 and 2.`}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6-s6",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Configuring Amazon Lex input",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"The Get customer input block supports voice input by integrating with Amazon Lex. With this integration, callers can provide input using their voice, eliminating the need for traditional keypad navigation. Amazon Lex accurately interprets the caller's spoken words and extracts relevant information, such as account numbers, names, or specific requests. You can access the Amazon Lex configuration settings by selecting the tab with the same name in the Get customer input block configuration panel."},{type:"p",text:"Get customer input configuration panel with the Amazon Lex option selected."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6-s7",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Configuring sentiment analysis",blocks:[{type:"h",level:4,text:"Flow branches for Amazon Lex"},{type:"p",text:"Choose Select a Lex bot, then choose the Name and Alias from the menus following this option. If you do not see your bot in the list, contact the cloud administrator and check the Amazon Connect instance configuration for Amazon Lex."},{type:"p",text:"Choose Add intent to define each Amazon Lex bot intent you want to implement a flow action for. The name of the intent configured in this panel must match the name of the Lex intents exactly."},{type:"p",text:"Now that you have reviewed selecting the bot and intents, move on to the next tab to learn about configuring sentiment analysis."},{type:"ul",items:["Get customer input configuration options showing Lex bot name and alias, and two intents.","Check your knowledge"]},{type:"callout",variant:"note",title:"What's next",body:["In this lesson, you reviewed options to capture contact input in Amazon Connect. In the next lesson, you will learn about Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex data exchange. Configuring sentiment analysis Flow branches for Amazon Lex Amazon Lex provides sentiment analysis results based on spoken words or utterances. This analysis focuses solely on the contact's most recent utterance, not the entire conversation history. You can use sentiment analysis in your flow to change the contact experience based on the value of the sentiment score. For example, if a contact interacts with the bot to book a hotel reservation, and the sentiment is mostly positive, you can continue offering self-service options. Otherwise, you might want to find a human agent that can handle the interaction more effectively. Now that you have reviewed configuring sentiment analysis, move on to the next tab to learn about flow branches for Amazon Lex. Flow branches for Amazon Lex When you save the configuration changes, the Get customer input block displays one exit for each condition configured. In this example, you see the BookHotel and BookCar intent exits and the positive and negative sentiment conditions exits. Additionally, the block has a default exit that is activated when none of the other exit conditions are met. The Error branch is activated when one of the configuration settings cannot be evaluated. For example, if you reference a dynamic attribute value that was not previously set in the flow, the Error branch activates. Lesson 8 of 12"]}]}],reviewQuestions:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6-q1",question:"A contact center administrator is designing a new contact flow for a service line. The flow should start with a personalized greeting, then provide menu options for different inquiries. Which block should an interactive voice response (IVR) designer use to capture the contact's selections?",options:[{id:"A",text:"Play prompt"},{id:"B",text:"Get Customer input"},{id:"C",text:"Transfer to queue"},{id:"D",text:"Invoke AWS Lambda function"}],correctOptionId:"B",rationale:"When using the Get customer input flow block, IVR designers gain access to the built-in integration between Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect. The block captures interactive and dynamic input from contacts. It supports interruptible prompts with dual tone multi-frequency (DTMF) input and Amazon Lex bot. This means that users can make selections using their telephone keypad or use natural language to state their intent."},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t6-q2",question:"An interactive voice response (IVR) designer wants to ensure that contacts provide their input using voice instead of traditional keypad navigation. Which AWS service provides this functionality through a built-in integration with Amazon Connect?",options:[{id:"A",text:"Amazon Polly"},{id:"B",text:"Amazon Transcribe"},{id:"C",text:"Amazon Lex"},{id:"D",text:"Amazon Comprehend"}],correctOptionId:"C",rationale:"When using the Get customer input flow block, IVR designers gain access to the built-in integration between Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect. The block captures interactive and dynamic input from contacts. It supports interruptible prompts with dual tone multi-frequency (DTMF) input and Amazon Lex bot. This means that users can make selections using their telephone keypad or use natural language to state their intent."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7",number:7,title:"Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex Data Exchange",shortTitle:"Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex Data Exchange",summary:"Amazon Connect's built-in integration with Amazon Lex reduces customer effort by offering natural language interactions, self-service options, and…",duration:"~7 min",lede:null,objectives:["Recognize how Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect exchange data.","Explore Amazon Lex session attributes."],sections:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-s1",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Lesson introduction",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Connect's built-in integration with Amazon Lex reduces customer effort by offering natural language interactions, self-service options, and effective routing. The integration makes it is possible for the two services to dynamically exchange information to solve for multiple use cases. In this lesson, you will explore the bidirectional data communication between Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-s2",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Data exchange overview",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"When a contact engages with an Amazon Connect contact center, IVR designers use flows to define the user experience. Contact information is stored as contact attributes."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-s3",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Amazon Connect to Amazon Lex",blocks:[{type:"h",level:4,text:"Amazon Lex to Amazon Connect"},{type:"p",text:"Amazon Connect passes contact information to Amazon Lex through the Get customer input block configuration."},{type:"p",text:"Developers can also programmatically pass information from Amazon Connect flows to Amazon Lex using Lambda functions."},{type:"p",text:"Both Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex can access common third-party data stores using Lambda functions."},{type:"p",text:"Now that you have reviewed Amazon Connect to Amazon Lex, move on to the next tab to learn about Amazon Lex to Amazon Connect."},{type:"p",text:"This integration empowers Amazon Connect to dynamically adjust the experience, route calls to the right queues or agents, offer personalized assistance, or run automated tasks."},{type:"p",text:"Throughout the interaction, Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect continuously exchange data. This real-time data exchange allows both services to maintain a comprehensive understanding of the contact's intent and preferences, and drive next actions. The exchange is possible by using Amazon Connect contact attributes and Amazon Lex session attributes."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-s4",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Use case",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"The following is a sample self-service flow that uses Amazon Connect's conversational chatbot and dynamic personalization. In this example, Nikki Wolf is a frequent flyer that uses AnyCompany Airline services for work and personal trips. Nikki's flight was cancelled due to extreme weather conditions. Nikki reaches out to the contact center using her mobile phone."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Cancelled flight"},{type:"p",text:"The Amazon Connect flow uses a search by phone number functionality to retrieve relevant information about the caller. The AnyCompany Airline IVR greets Nikki by name and thanks her for being a frequent flyer."},{type:"p",text:"Instead of asking Nikki to select options, the Amazon Connect IVR uses the cancelled flight information from her profile and anticipates Nikki's intent. Nikki is happy she does not need to explain herself, nor go through a series of menu selections."},{type:"p",text:"Nikki confirms that this is the reason for her call and asks if there is any flight available within 24 hours. The IVR searches for alternative flight options and finds a flight later that day and one the next day at the same time."},{type:"p",text:"First, the IVR apologizes, then presents the flight choices. Nikki prefers the same flight tomorrow."},{type:"p",text:"By using Nikki's information, the IVR runs through a series of confirmations. The IVR successfully books the flight."},{type:"p",text:"Based on Nikki's preferred channel of contact, the IVR follows up with an SMS message. The message contains the confirmation number and a link to her updated flight details."},{type:"p",text:"This scenario demonstrates a successful resolution of a complex use case that does not require agent intervention. This implementation is possible due to the dynamic exchange of data between Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-s5",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Amazon Lex session attributes",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Amazon Connect is a client application that initiates sessions with Amazon Lex using the Get customer input flow block. Developers have an option to include a chatbot in a conversation by using the Amazon Lex APIs. Amazon Lex session attributes are key value pairs that store and carry information throughout the duration of a session. Session attributes contain application-specific information that is passed between a bot and a client application during a session. Amazon Lex maintains context and personalizes responses based on user input and the logic implemented for various types of conversations."},{type:"p",text:"Similar to the Amazon Connect contact attributes, Amazon Lex session attributes store user-specific data, or contextual details relevant to the ongoing conversation. Amazon Lex session attributes are dynamic and can be modified based on user inputs, system actions, or external data sources. This flexibility allows Amazon Lex to dynamically adapt responses and actions for contacts."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-s6",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Setting Amazon Lex attributes from Amazon Connect",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Session attributes in Amazon Lex play an important role in managing conversation state and flow. They help track the progression of the conversation, manage branching logic, and select appropriate actions. For example, you can configure the maximum duration of utterances, the maximum length of a numeric input, or an end character for DTMF input."},{type:"p",text:"To assign values to Amazon Lex session attributes, use the Get customer input block configuration panel in the Amazon Connect flow. Select the Amazon Lex tab, then choose Add an attribute for each attribute you want to set."},{type:"p",text:"The following image shows the configuration for two Lex session attributes, phone_number and x-amz-lex:audio:start-timeout-ms:*.*."},{type:"p",text:"Customer input block configuration panel showing two session attributes, phone_number and a system variable for start timeout. The phone_number attribute contains contact information, and the system variable manages system behavior."},{type:"p",text:"Learn more about common Amazon Lex session attributes you can change while designing an IVR bot."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Interrupt behavior"},{type:"p",text:"This defines whether the user can interrupt the prompt that solicits input. You can selectively turn it off by setting the value of the attribute to False."},{type:"p",text:"The interrupt functionality is also referred to as barge-in, and it is enabled by default. You can disable it in the Amazon Lex console. You can also modify the barge-in behavior by using the following allow-interrupt session attribute."},{type:"p",text:"x-amz-lex:allow-interrupt:<intentName>:<slotName>"},{type:"p",text:"Default value: True."},{type:"p",text:"To configure interrupts for all intents and slots, replace the <intentName> and <slotName> with an asterisk:"},{type:"p",text:"x-amz-lex:allow-interrupt:*:*"},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Maximum utterance length"},{type:"p",text:"This defines how long the bot waits before speech input is truncated and the speech is returned to your application. You can increase the length of the input when you expect long responses, or if you want to give customers more time to provide information. To do so, use the following attribute, and set the value for your configuration setting."},{type:"ul",items:["x-amz-lex:audio:max-length-ms:<intentName>:<slotName>","Default value: 13,000 milliseconds (13 seconds). The maximum value is 15,000 milliseconds (15 seconds)"]},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Voice timeout"},{type:"p",text:"Defines how long a bot waits before assuming that the customer isn't going to speak. You can increase the time in situations where the customer might need more time to find or recall information before speaking. For example, you might want to give customers time to get out their credit card so they can enter the number."},{type:"p",text:"x-amz-lex:audio:start-timeout-ms:<intentName>:<slotName>"}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-s7",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Default value: 4,000 milliseconds (4 seconds)",blocks:[{type:"h",level:4,text:"Configuration for DTMF input"},{type:"p",text:"Here are some features of this attribute:"},{type:"ul",items:["Allow DTMF input: You can set the type of input that the bot can accept using session attributes.","x-amz-lex:allow-dtmf-input:"]},{type:"p",text:"<intentName>:<slotName>"}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-s8",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Default value: True",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"End character: This is the DTMF character that immediately ends input. If the user does not press this character, the input ends after the end timeout."},{type:"p",text:"x-amz-lex:dtmf:end-character:"},{type:"p",text:"<intentName>:<slotName>"},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Default value: #"},{type:"p",text:"To discover all Amazon Lex attributes and their default values, navigate to the Configuring timeouts for capturing user input documentation section."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-s9",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Accessing Amazon Lex attributes from Amazon Connect",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Organizations can use Lex session attributes to enhance the overall experience and streamline interactions within Amazon Connect. To reference an attribute from an Amazon Lex bot, you use the format:"},{type:"ul",items:["$.Lex.","$.Lex. and include the part of the Amazon Lex bot to reference. For example, $.Lex.IntentName."]},{type:"p",text:"To reference the customer input to an Amazon Lex bot slot, use $.Lex.Slots.slotName."},{type:"ul",items:["$.Lex.Slots.slotName.","Replace slotName with the name of the slot configured in the bot."]},{type:"p",text:"For a list of Amazon Lex attributes, navigate to the Amazon Lex contact attributes section of the Amazon Connect Administrator Guide."},{type:"p",text:"For a list of blocks with access to Lex attributes, navigate to the How flow blocks use Lex session attributes section of the Amazon Connect Administrator Guide."},{type:"callout",variant:"note",title:"What's next",body:["In this lesson, you have explored how data exchanges between Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex. Continue to the next lesson to review the course summary and prepare for the end-of-course assessment. Amazon Lex to Amazon Connect Amazon Lex uses intents and slots to store and process user input. Amazon Lex accesses external sources such as databases, APIs, or third-party services to fulfill the user's request. After Amazon Lex fulfills the user intent, it returns the control and user data to Amazon Connect. Amazon Connect uses Amazon Lex data and implements the next actions based on the received information. Lesson 9 of 12"]}]}],reviewQuestions:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-q1",question:"A company wants to use Amazon Lex session attributes to store contact preferences and past interactions for personalized responses. Which statement accurately describes the role of session attributes in Amazon Lex?",options:[{id:"A",text:"Session attributes are static variables that cannot be modified during a conversation."},{id:"B",text:"Session attributes can only store customer account information and cannot hold contextual details."},{id:"C",text:"Session attributes are dynamic variables that can store user-specific data, and update based on user inputs and system logic."},{id:"D",text:"Session attributes are used solely for managing conversation flow and cannot store user preferences or past interactions."}],correctOptionId:"C",rationale:"Amazon Lex session attributes store user-specific data or contextual details relevant to the ongoing conversation. Amazon Lex session attributes are dynamic and can be modified based on user inputs, system logic, or external data sources. This flexibility allows Amazon Lex to dynamically adapt responses and actions for contacts."},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t7-q2",question:"A company allows the users to interrupt the bot prompt mid-sentence. This way, they can provide input without waiting for the bot to finish speaking. Which Amazon Lex session attribute ensures this functionality is available?",options:[{id:"A",text:"x-amz-lex:audio:start-timeout-ms:*:* set to 0, so the bot does not speak"},{id:"B",text:"x-amz-lex:allow-interrupt:*:* session attribute set to True to allow interruption for all intents and slots"},{id:"C",text:"x-amz-lex:audio:max-length-ms:*:* set to 15 seconds to extend the duration of the utterance"},{id:"D",text:"The x-amz-lex:allow-dtmf-input:*:* set to True to offer dual tone multi-frequency (DTMF) interruptible DTMF input"}],correctOptionId:"B",rationale:"The interrupt functionality is also referred to as barge-in and it is enabled by default. You can disable it in the Amazon Lex console. You can also modify the barge-in behavior by using the allow-interrupt session attribute."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t8",number:8,title:"Summary",shortTitle:"Summary",summary:"Organizations can offer enhanced self-service capabilities, intelligent call routing, and personalized customer experiences. This is possible with…",duration:"~2 min",lede:null,objectives:[],sections:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t8-s1",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Course summary",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Organizations can offer enhanced self-service capabilities, intelligent call routing, and personalized customer experiences. This is possible with the integration of Amazon Lex conversational bots into Amazon Connect. Review the summary of each main topic covered in this course to prepare for the final assessment."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Overview"},{type:"p",text:"IVR is an automated telephony solution that uses voice and touch-tone inputs to streamline operations, enhance efficiency, and improve experiences. Traditional IVR relied on DTMF input, where callers navigated through menus by pressing numbers on their phone's keypad. In contrast, modern IVR systems use advanced speech recognition and NLP technologies, giving callers the ability to interact using conversational language."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"IVR design considerations"},{type:"p",text:"Effective IVR design requires careful attention to various factors, including menu clarity, automation compared to human intervention, customization, and accessibility. Clarity and ease of use of menu options, personalization and customization, and ensuring accessibility for individuals with disabilities are crucial considerations. Deciding between using conversational bots or DTMF input depends on different factors. Consider interaction complexity, user preferences, flexibility requirements, scalability considerations, integration needs, and the desired level of contextual understanding when designing experiences."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Capturing contact input"},{type:"p",text:"Within the Amazon Connect contact flow editor, administrators design the IVR menu by adding blocks like Play prompt, Get customer input, or Transfer to queue. The Get customer input block prompts callers to provide specific information or make selections using their keypad or voice. Administrators can configure various input types, validation rules, and conditional logic to personalize the call experience based on the customer's input."},{type:"h",level:4,text:"Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex data exchange"},{type:"p",text:"When a contact engages with an Amazon Connect flow that is integrated with Amazon Lex, their input is captured and transmitted to Amazon Lex. It employs predefined intents and slots to process the customer's input, identifying the purpose of the interaction and any specific details provided. Amazon Lex formulates a response, which is forwarded to Amazon Connect, enabling dynamic adjustment of the IVR experience. It also assists with call routing, personalized assistance, or automated task performance based on the conversation context. Throughout the interaction, Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect exchange data in real time, ensuring updated information and context retention."}]}],reviewQuestions:[]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t9",number:9,title:"Course summary",shortTitle:"Course summary",summary:"Interactive Voice Response (IVR) are automated telephony solutions that leverage voice and touch-tone inputs to streamline operations, enhance…",duration:"~2 min",lede:null,objectives:[],sections:[{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t9-s1",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Amazon ConnectConversational InterfacesIntermediate",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Interactive Voice Response (IVR) are automated telephony solutions that leverage voice and touch-tone inputs to streamline operations, enhance efficiency, and improve experiences. Traditional IVR relied on Dual-Tone Multi-Frequency (DTMF) input, where callers navigated through menus by pressing numbers on their phone's keypad. In contrast, modern IVR systems utilize advanced speech recognition and natural language processing (NLP) technologies, allowing callers to interact using conversational language."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t9-s2",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"IVR Design Considerations",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Effective IVR design requires careful attention to various factors, including menu clarity, automation versus human intervention, customization, and accessibility. Clarity and simplicity of menu options, personalization and customization, and ensuring accessibility for individuals with disabilities are crucial considerations."},{type:"p",text:"Deciding between using conversational bots or Dual Tone Multi-Frequency (DTMF) input depends on different factors. Consider interaction complexity, user preferences, flexibility requirements, scalability considerations, integration needs, and the desired level of contextual understanding when designing experiences."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t9-s3",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Capturing Contact Input",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"Within the Amazon Connect contact flow editor, administrators design the IVR menu by adding blocks like Play prompt, Get customer input, or Transfer to queue. The Get Customer Input block prompts callers to provide specific information or make selections using their keypad or voice. Administrators can configure various input types, validation rules, and conditional logic to personalize the call experience based on the customer's input."}]},{id:"connect-conversational-interfaces-t9-s4",eyebrow:null,duration:null,title:"Amazon Connect and Amazon Lex Data Exchange",blocks:[{type:"p",text:"When a contact engages with an Amazon Connect Flow integrated with Amazon Lex, their input is captured and transmitted to Amazon Lex. It employs predefined intents and slots to process the customer's input, identifying the purpose of the interaction and any specific details provided. Amazon Lex formulates a response, which is forwarded to Amazon Connect, enabling dynamic adjustment of the IVR experience. It also assists with call routing, personalized assistance, or automated task execution based on the conversation context. Throughout the interaction, Amazon Lex and Amazon Connect exchange data in real- time, ensuring updated information and context retention."}]}],reviewQuestions:[]}],quiz:null};export{e as course};
